 succeeded in 0ms:
    14	
    15	## 1. The flow
    16	
    17	```
    18	THE CEO'S JOB, in one sentence he would sign
    19	   │
    20	   ├── small — a fix, a deletion, a text he already worded: no plan, straight to ARRANGE
    21	   │
    22	PLAN      the talk turns to plan, design or architecture → `plan`: effort warning MAX (§2)
    23	          the plan goes to him once, in his language, closing with the HIGH warning (§2) → his yes
    24	   │
    25	ARRANGE   the lead chooses the hands (§3) and says it in one line
    26	   │
    27	BUILD     the hands write and run their own tests; the lead verifies every piece and commits it
    28	   │
    29	SOL       one blind, read-only pass over the job's commits (§4) — A blocks · B fixed now · C in the report
    30	   │
    31	FIX       a fork or a writing helper fixes; the lead verifies; no second Sol round
    32	   │
    33	TEST      the touched tests; code changed → typecheck and the battery once (§5)
    34	   │
    35	COMMIT the fixes → TELL him in a few lines (§6) → STATE, when nothing is left in hand
    36	```
    37	
    38	**What reaches him as a question:** starting a job, its plan (once), money out, a contract, an identity
    39	step, the Islamic boundaries, a discovery that would change the scope he approved, the architecture or
    40	the data's integrity (the work stops at it), and an order of his that truly reads two ways — each with a
    41	recommendation, answerable in one word. Technical choices inside a job he said yes to are the lead's.
    42	The plan's single trip is his word <!-- CEO-OK: plan-comes-to-him-once-2026-10-03 -->.
    43	
    44	**The plan**, when there is one, names the scope, what it touches, the ordered steps, the
    45	arrangement, how it is verified, and the risks — in his language, short. No file changes before his yes.
    46	
    47	## 2. Effort — the session warns, he switches
    48	
    49	The session runs at `high`: the talk, the build, the fixes, the tests. Plan, design and architecture run
    50	at `max`. **He switches with /effort; the lead warns him — on every reply, until he has.**
    51	
    52	- The moment the talk turns to a plan, a design or an architecture, the lead runs
    53	  `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py plan`; it answers with the live level and whether this reply
    54	  opens with `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan konuşmasındayız — /effort max'a geçin.` (not when he is already at
    55	  max). His plan permission mode starts the same warning by itself.
    56	- In the reply that brings him the plan for his yes, the lead runs
    57	  `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py build` and closes that reply with
    58	  `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan bitti — koda geçmeden /effort high'a geçin.` — the warning travels with the
    59	  question, so his yes can arrive at high and the build starts at once. A yes that arrives at another
    60	  level gets the line again, and nothing is written until he has switched; a correction instead of a yes
    61	  → `plan` again. At the job's end: `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py off`.
    62	- Between those, the hook `dxb-effort-warn.py` repeats the line on each of his messages until the
    63	  session's live level matches. With no mode set, a message of his naming a plan, a design or an
    64	  architecture reminds the lead to run `plan`, and a session whose level is not high reminds the lead to
    65	  open any reply that writes code with `⚠ Muhittin Bey, koda geçmeden /effort high'a geçin.` — a small
    66	  job is not written at max either.
    67	- Measured 2026-10-06: neither a hook's input nor its environment carries the effort (`CLAUDE_EFFORT`
    68	  reaches the Bash tool, not a UserPromptSubmit hook). The live level is the newest, by timestamp, of the
    69	  status line's record (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/claude-ctx/<session>.json`, field `effort`), this session's last
    70	  step in the transcript, and its last /effort row there (`Set effort level to …`, written by a switch
    71	  between turns; a switch in the middle of a turn writes none) — none known → an unknown level.
    72	  A skill's `effort` can raise a turn but never lower one — the reason the old design-at-max skill went.
    73	
    74	## 3. The seats and the arrangement
    75	
    76	| Seat | Who | Does | Never |
    77	|---|---|---|---|
    78	| **Lead** | the session, `high` | chooses the hands, writes what it keeps, verifies every piece and every fix, runs the tests, commits, tells him | approves its own work; commits a piece it has not verified; asks him a technical question |
    79	| **Fork** | the lead's own copy (`subagent_type: "fork"`), its whole context | writes a large piece that needs this conversation | commits |
    80	| **Writing helper** | `helper-writer` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`) | writes and tests a piece the lead specified; fixes a Sol finding | commits; widens the piece <!-- CEO-OK: helpers-write-code-under-lead-verification-2026-10-03 --> |
    81	| **Helper** | `helper` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`, read-only) | reads code or documents, measures, researches | writes |
    82	| **Reader** | a one-shot `scout` or `Explore` | a wide search whose text would swell the lead's context | writes; is resumed (its cache dies at 5 min) |
    83	| **Escalation writer** | `builder` (Opus 5.5 · `max`), one-shot | only a piece that failed twice at `high` | is opened for a routine fix |
    84	| **Auditor** | GPT-6.1 Sol <!-- CEO-OK: auditor-sol-6-1-2026-10-01 -->, through `scripts/governance/refuter.sh` | one blind pass per job (§4) | writes; audits a job twice; sees the lead's reasoning or verdict |
    85	
    86	Fable 5.1 is not consulted in a dxb-team2 job — neither the `advisor` tool (its own "before the work
    87	and before done" advice does not apply here) nor the Fable-based `design-eye`.
    88	
    89	**The arrangement — chosen at ARRANGE, said to him in one line with its why**
    90	<!-- CEO-OK: orchestration-three-arrangements-lead-chooses-2026-10-03 -->:
    91	
    92	| Arrangement | Who writes | When |
    93	|---|---|---|
    94	| **Lead** | the lead itself | a small job, or pieces so coupled that splitting them costs more than it saves |
    95	| **Fork** | the fork | one large piece that needs the whole conversation, while the lead stays free to verify |
    96	| **Team** | `helper-writer`s, in parallel; `helper`s read and research beside them | independent pieces, each specifiable in a paragraph |
    97	| **Hybrid** | the fork writes the core; `helper-writer`s the side pieces; `helper`s read | a large core with independent pieces around it |
    98	
    99	- Parallel writers never touch the same file, and run their tests with `DXB_ENGINE_LOCK_WAIT=900`: one
   100	  run at a time holds the construction engine, and with it a second one queues instead of being refused.
   101	- Each writer gets a spec it can work from alone: the files, the
   102	  behaviour, the measured facts it must not re-litigate, the test it writes first, what it reports.
   103	- The lead verifies each piece before it is committed — reads the diff, runs its tests — and commits
   104	  only what it verified. Nobody else commits.
   105	- A fork starts cheap: its first call reads the lead's cache (203,676 tokens read, 1,200 written —
   106	  session 5ed74ad7, 2026-10-03).
   107	- A piece that fails: a medium writer's goes to the lead or a fork at `high`; one that failed twice at
   108	  `high` goes to the `builder`, never earlier. Opus `xhigh` is never used for code (FrontierCode 51.4 %, the bottom of the
   109	  curve); `max` writes code only as the escalation writer — a medium try plus a high retry (USD 1.89)
   110	  is under a third of one `max` run (USD 6.19) and passes the same auditor. Prices in this door are
   111	  written USD, never with a dollar sign: opened with a job sentence, a dollar sign and a digit are
   112	  replaced by the sentence's words (measured 2026-10-04).
   113	
   114	## 4. Sol — one blind pass
   115	
   116	Every job that changes code, a rule or a door gets one Sol pass, after BUILD; a change to the records
   117	alone (STATE, the board) is checked by the rulers on commit instead.
   118	
   119	- **Launch:** `scripts/governance/refuter.sh [--effort medium|high|xhigh] "<brief>"` — `high` by
   120	  default; `xhigh` for money, a database, security, approval, governance or the agents; `medium` for a
   121	  text-only change. An audit can outlast the Bash tool's 10 minutes: launch it detached (a small
   122	  script under `setsid nohup`, its output to the job folder, `EXIT=` on its last line) and wait for
   123	  that line.
   124	- **The brief — blind, not in the dark:** his words verbatim; the plan's decisions as facts ("this door
   125	  is closed on purpose"); the range; where the raw test output lies. Never the lead's reasoning, a
   126	  summary, "tests are green", or another auditor's verdict. Its first question: *does this do what he
   127	  asked?*
   128	- **Its hand:** Sol queries the construction engine itself through its one tool `sql_read`
   129	  (`scripts/governance/sol-db-mcp.mjs`, role `sol_reader`) — the lead never hands over a count Sol can
   130	  take. Its sandbox has no network, so the lead runs the database-writing tests and hands their output
   131	  over whole.
   132	- **Its findings**, per claim: Claim · Verdict (REFUTED | STANDS AFTER ATTEMPTED REFUTATION |
   133	  UNVERIFIED) · Finding (`file:line`, scenario, impact) · Evidence · Correction required.
   134	  **A** blocks · **B** is fixed in this pass · **C** is older than this work and stays in the job's
   135	  report — never the board; a row opens only on his word.
   136	- **Fix:** a fork or a `helper-writer` fixes each A and B; the lead verifies the finding's own case.
   137	  There is no second Sol round <!-- CEO-OK: sol-single-pass-fixes-by-helper-2026-10-03 -->.
   138	- **Dispute:** the lead runs the evidence Sol gave — it fails → real, fixed; it holds → the finding drops
   139	  and the report says why. A question no test can settle is the lead's, with its reason in the report.
   140	- **Sol unavailable** (quota, error, timeout): on an ordinary job the `refuter` subagent (Opus 5.5 ·
   141	  `high`) audits once, and the report says *"audited by Opus instead of Sol — read-only by word, not by
   142	  tool — because …"*; on money, a database or security the lead waits for Sol's window and works on
   143	  something else. No job closes unaudited; the auditor is never swapped silently
   144	  <!-- CEO-OK: fallback-auditor-named-and-refuter-second-round-gone-2026-10-04 -->.
   145	
   146	## 5. Tests
   147	
   148	- New behaviour is proven by a test written first — red before the code, green after.
   149	- After each piece, its own tests. After Sol's fixes, the touched tests again.
   150	- **Code changed → `pnpm typecheck`, then the battery, once, at the end** — and a resident service whose
   151	  code changed is restarted (`dxb-verify`). The typecheck covers `packages/` and `apps/`, not `tests/` or
   152	  `db/seed/` (measured 2026-10-06). The battery: `pnpm construction:battery`, the whole suite in
   153	  both halves against the construction's own engine (port 54422), 1,486 tests, about 6 minutes
   154	  (2026-10-04). Launch it detached from a shell whose `oom_score_adj` is 100; green is the line
   155	  `BATTERY_GREEN`. It answers the one question no single test can: did this break anything else.
   156	- **Text only** → no battery. A door, the core or a record: the commit hook's rulers read those on
   157	  every commit. A persona's words: `bash scripts/persona-ruler.sh` (dxb-verify), which the commit
   158	  hook does not run.
   159	- The commit hook runs by itself on every commit: the secret scan (gitleaks); the records ruler when
   160	  STATE, the board or the ledger change; the opening ruler when the core, a skill or the opening hook
   161	  change; the research ruler and `tests/b46` when the research engine changes.
   162	
   163	## 6. Commit, tell, record
   164	
   165	- **Commit:** one per job, or one per independent piece; the message says what changed and why.
   166	- **Tell him** in his language, in a few lines, under three headings — *Sizi bekleyen · Değişen ·
   167	  Bulunan* <!-- CEO-OK: job-report-three-headings-2026-10-03 -->: what waits on him; what changed;
   168	  what was found outside the job, each with a recommendation. An empty heading is not written; a
   169	  defect fixed inside the job is not listed; the proof stays in the work until he asks.
   170	- **His words** are written once, in `scripts/governance/ceo-approvals.json`, and only where a record
   171	  must rest on his order or approval (LAW B) — never copied into commits, doors or STATE.
   172	- **STATE** is written once, when the job is told and nothing is left in hand (a session's end cannot
   173	  be seen coming): the three headings, a few lines each — what
   174	  finished and waits for his eye, what is next, what waits on him. No conversation, no quotes, no
   175	  hashes or counts beyond the one that names a job.
   176	- **The job folder** (`.planning/quick/<date>-<slug>/`) holds Sol's report and the raw evidence — only
   177	  for a job that went to Sol.
   178	
   179	## 7. Long commands, context and handover
   180	
   181	- **The 4-minute rules** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-cost-gate.py`): a command over 4 minutes is the
   182	  lead's; a subagent idle over 4 minutes is never resumed — its cache is the 5-minute one.
   183	- **The context gate** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-context-gate.py`) and **the clean break**: from
   184	  50 % used, hand over at the first clean break — the piece finished well, the tree committed, no
   185	  battery running, the report sent — even when reaching it takes the session to about 60 %; never in
   186	  the middle of a piece. The clean break and the quality decide, not the number; the lead judges it,
   187	  helpers included <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-the-clean-break-2026-10-06 -->. At 60 % the gate refuses
   188	  the Agent tool — a safety net, past which nothing new starts.
   189	- **The handover** is done by the engineer, never the CEO
   190	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-50-without-asking-2026-10-01 -->. A note in the scratchpad, in the author's
   191	  own voice: the job's place in the whole first, then his words of this job verbatim, every phase with
   192	  status and commit, the traps met, the first message the successor sends; session-only orders marked
   193	  as such; last, the path of this session's own conversation —
   194	  `~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl` — with the line *if
   195	  anything here is unclear, read the part you need there*
   196	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-carries-transcript-2026-10-03 -->, `wl-copy`'d. He is quoted verbatim — from
   197	  the ledger, or from this conversation marked so
   198	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-quotes-conversation-verbatim-2026-10-03 --> — never composed in his first
   199	  person. The successor opens with the same model at his saved default effort, in a VS Code
   200	  editor-area terminal, through `operator` (look first; if the CEO is typing, wait):
   201	  `operator key ctrl+shift+p` → `operator type "Terminal: Create New Terminal in Editor Area"` →
   202	  `operator key Return` → `operator shot` → paste with `operator key ctrl+shift+v` (`ctrl+v` does not
   203	  reach the terminal; `operator type` inverts case here, so a command line is pasted, never typed) the
   204	  line
   205	  `systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect -p MemoryMax=16G -p MemorySwapMax=4G -- /home/dxb/.local/bin/claude --model <same> "$(cat <note>)"`
   206	  (`claude` is not on that terminal's PATH) → `operator shot`, read it → `operator key Return`.
   207	  Proof it is alive: `ListAgents` shows it and it answers. If the job is in its plan phase, the
   208	  successor's first reply carries the max warning (§2).
